#!/usr/bin/env python3
# version: 1
"""herdr-usage: what the AI providers a machine uses have left, read on that machine.

Herdr for Android copies it to a machine over SSH and runs it there, so the phone sees each
provider's limits without any key leaving the machine. It reads:

  - Claude Code (subscription): the 5-hour session and weekly limits, from Anthropic's usage API with
    Claude Code's own login (~/.claude/.credentials.json, or the macOS keychain).
  - Codex (subscription): its 5-hour and weekly limits, from ChatGPT's usage API with Codex's own
    login (~/.codex/auth.json); else the newest limits Codex wrote in its sessions.
  - API accounts whose keys are already on the machine (the environment, pi's and opencode's auth
    files): DeepSeek's balance, OpenRouter's spend and limit, Anthropic's costs (with an admin key),
    OpenCode Go's key and its limits estimated from what this machine's agents spent.
  - Local models (llama.cpp, Ollama, LM Studio …, from pi's models.json): whether they answer.
  - What the agents used, per day, board task and agent, from their own session records (Claude
    Code, Codex, pi, omp, opencode) over the last week.

Nothing is changed and no login is renewed: an expired one says so (its tool renews it when it runs).
Readings are cached (~/.cache/herdr-usage): the providers are asked at most every few minutes, and the
session records are read on from where the last look stopped.

  herdr-usage read [--fresh]     everything, as one JSON line
  herdr-usage limits [--fresh]   the limits alone (what herdr-push warns about)
  herdr-usage version

Python 3.6 or newer alone, no packages. See host/herdr-usage/README.md in the herdr-android repo.
"""

